    "q-99839": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99839",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-5t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )}{6})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
